{
  "esm": "0.8.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "latlon3d_advection_meridional_upwind_flux",
    "description": "Method-of-manufactured-solutions driver for the FLUX-FORM first-order-upwind MERIDIONAL advection operator grids/latlon3d/rules/upwind1_flux_D_lat_polar, i.e. the spherical flux divergence (1/(a cos phi)) d/dphi ( cos(phi) v q ) with the wind INSIDE the operator. The transport equation is du/dt = -D(V*u, wrt: lat) + src, and the rule's compound match D(V*q, wrt: lat) lowers the whole product. This is the meridional sibling of problems/latlon3d_advection_vertical_upwind_flux, and the FIRST consumer anywhere in the library of the grids/latlon half-polar face geometry: it declares the observed phie = lat_edge() over [lat_nodes] and feeds it to coslat_edge and latcell_area to build the two free names the rule reads -- coslat_e (face length) and dS_lat (exact spherical cell area) -- exactly as the vertical drivers build dz from lev_thickness. \n\nWHY THE WIND VANISHES AT THE POLES, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A DODGE. The operator's truncation error scales as O(dphi / cos(phi)): the donor-cell face reconstruction is O(dphi) and row j divides it by a cell area of O(cos(phi_j) dphi) (at the half-polar caps, by O(dphi^2)). The measured consequences are set out in full in the rule's ACCURACY AT THE POLES section -- at any FIXED latitude the scheme is cleanly first order; the AREA-WEIGHTED error is first order, because the O(cos phi) mass weight cancels the 1/cos(phi) exactly; but the UNWEIGHTED Linf over the WHOLE SPHERE cannot converge for a general 2-D wind, because the pole-adjacent row always sits at cos(phi) ~ dphi and so carries O(1) error at EVERY resolution. That is the lat-lon coordinate singularity, not a defect of the discretization, and nothing local to the spatial operator removes it (a true polar cap and a higher-order reconstruction were both measured and both fail; the operational remedy is a runtime polar filter). \n\nThe conformance harness gates EVERY norm in its manifest and its L2/Linf are UNWEIGHTED, so this driver uses a wind whose meridional component VANISHES AT THE POLES like cos(phi): V(i,j,k) = coslat_e[j] a(i) b(k). Then the product V dq/dphi -> 0 like cos(phi), the 1/cos(phi) amplification is cancelled pointwise, and the raw Linf converges at order 1 over the whole sphere. MEASURED on the pure operator over NLAT = 17/33/65/129: Linf(all) 1.01/1.01/1.00, Linf(interior) 1.01/1.01/1.00, Linf(caps) 1.99/2.00/2.00 -- the caps converge at SECOND order, because there the cos(phi) wind is itself O(dphi). MEASURED on this driver at t = 0.1 (the gated sweep): L2 pairwise 0.98/0.99/0.99 and Linf pairwise 0.99/1.01/1.00. This is a legitimate verification case -- it verifies that the operator implements the intended discretization -- and it is the case a physical model actually runs, since a wind field that is smooth as a VECTOR on the sphere has v -> 0 at the poles unless it carries a first zonal harmonic there. What this driver deliberately does NOT do is dress the general-wind behaviour up as convergent by loosening the order tolerance; that behaviour is measured, documented in the rule, and left documented. \n\nTHE WIND REVERSES SIGN WITH HEIGHT. b(k) = cos(pi (k-1)/(NLEV-1)) is +1, +1/2, -1/2, -1 on the four levels, so the flow is NORTHWARD in the lower half of the column and SOUTHWARD in the upper half, and BOTH branches of the donor-cell selection are exercised in every column. Because the operator is purely meridional, level k is an independent 1-D problem whose wind is the same cos(phi) profile scaled by the constant a(i) b(k): reversing the sign swaps which neighbour donates but leaves the O(dphi) reconstruction error, and hence the order, unchanged -- which the sweep confirms rung by rung. The zonal modulation a(i) = 1 + 0.25 cos(2 pi (i-1/2)/NLON) is strictly positive and makes the rank-3 shape real: a stencil that mis-indexed V as a latitude profile rather than as a full [lon, lat_nodes, lev] field would be caught rather than silently agreeing. \n\nFORCED-STEADY (TRUNCATION) IDIOM, following the sibling latlon3d_advection_vertical_upwind_flux. The exact solution q*(phi) = 2 + sin(phi) is STEADY, u is seeded with its EXACT cos-weighted cell mean, and src is the EXACT flux divergence, so du/dt at t = 0 IS the truncation error and the drift at t = 0.1 measures it. Two closed forms make that spellable inside an `ic` -- which folds BUILD-TIME from literals plus metaparameter NAMES, with the model's own parameters and observeds OUT of scope, so neither dS_lat nor phie may be referenced there. (1) The exact cos-weighted cell mean of 2 + sin(phi), ( N(phi_e[j+1]) - N(phi_e[j]) ) / dS_lat[j] with N = 2 sin + sin^2/2, TELESCOPES to the cancellation-free 2 + (sin(phi_e[j]) + sin(phi_e[j+1]))/2 -- which is also what keeps the seed accurate at the caps, where the exact-integral form divides an O(dphi^2) numerator by an O(dphi^2) area weight. (2) The exact face flux Phi = V cos(phi) q* = (1 - s^2)(2 + s) a(i) b(k), s = sin(phi_e), is written as a POLYNOMIAL IN sin(phi_e) and is therefore EXACTLY 0.0 in IEEE at both poles -- sin(+/-pi/2) rounds to exactly +/-1.0, so 1 - s^2 is exactly 0.0 -- whereas cos(+/-pi/2) is 6.1e-17, NOT zero. src is thus a single aggregate that reproduces the rule's STRUCTURAL POLE-TERM OMISSION bitwise (x - 0.0 = x and 0.0 - x = -x are exact in IEEE) rather than merely to within 1e-33.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:problem",
      "grid:latlon3d",
      "op:advection",
      "scheme:upwind",
      "form:flux",
      "bc:no-flux",
      "axes:lon,lat,lev"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "LeVeque, R. J. (2002). Finite Volume Methods for Hyperbolic Problems. Cambridge University Press. Section 4.1 (the donor-cell flux)."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Lin, S.-J., & Rood, R. B. (1996). Multidimensional flux-form semi-Lagrangian transport schemes. Monthly Weather Review, 124(9), 2046-2070.",
        "doi": "10.1175/1520-0493(1996)124<2046:MFFSLT>2.0.CO;2"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Roache, P. J. (2002). Code verification by the method of manufactured solutions. Journal of Fluids Engineering, 124(1), 4-10.",
        "doi": "10.1115/1.1436090"
      }
    ]
  },
  "models": {
    "MeridionalAdvectionUpwindFlux3d": {
      "expression_template_imports": [
        {
          "ref": "../grids/latlon3d/rules/upwind1_flux_D_lat_polar.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLON": 8,
            "NLEV": 4
          }
        }
      ],
      "variables": {
        "u": {
          "type": "state",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Advected tracer; cell means. The exact steady solution is the cos-weighted cell mean of q*(phi) = 2 + sin(phi), which telescopes to 2 + (sin(phi_e[j]) + sin(phi_e[j+1]))/2."
        },
        "lat0_deg": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "deg",
          "default": -90.0,
          "description": "Southern domain edge; the grid's lat0_deg free name. -90 makes the recipe the GLOBAL pole-to-pole one, whose two end cells are the half-polar caps."
        },
        "dlat_deg": {
          "type": "observed",
          "units": "deg",
          "description": "Meridional point spacing 180/(NLAT-1) (pole-to-pole, endpoint-inclusive): the grid's dlat_deg free name. NLAT is the document's live metaparameter, so this follows every rebinding.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "/",
            "args": [
              180,
              {
                "op": "-",
                "args": [
                  "NLAT",
                  1
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "phie": {
          "type": "observed",
          "units": "deg",
          "shape": [
            "lat_nodes"
          ],
          "description": "Latitude cell EDGES in degrees over the NLAT+1 face axis, from the grid's lat_edge template (clamped-midpoint rule; aggregate index `gje`). phie[1] = -90 and phie[NLAT+1] = +90 EXACTLY, and the two end cells are half width -- the GEOS-Chem half-polar caps.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "apply_expression_template",
            "args": [],
            "name": "lat_edge",
            "bindings": {}
          }
        },
        "coslat_e": {
          "type": "observed",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lat_nodes"
          ],
          "description": "FACE-LENGTH weight cos(phi_e) over the face axis, from the grid's coslat_edge template (aggregate index `gjc`). One of the two free names the flux-form lat rules read. At the two POLE faces this is cos(-/+pi/2) = 6.1e-17 in IEEE, NOT zero -- which is exactly why those rules OMIT the pole-wall flux terms rather than multiplying them by it.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "apply_expression_template",
            "args": [],
            "name": "coslat_edge",
            "bindings": {
              "phie": "phie"
            }
          }
        },
        "dS_lat": {
          "type": "observed",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lat"
          ],
          "description": "EXACT spherical cell-AREA weight sin(phi_e[j+1]) - sin(phi_e[j]) over the cell axis, from the grid's latcell_area template (aggregate index `gja`). The other free name the flux-form lat rules read. Exact, not a midpoint approximation, which is what makes the flux divergence telescope to an exact global mass budget.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "apply_expression_template",
            "args": [],
            "name": "latcell_area",
            "bindings": {
              "phie": "phie"
            }
          }
        },
        "V": {
          "type": "observed",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat_nodes",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "THREE-DIMENSIONAL face-staggered MERIDIONAL wind, positive northward: V[i,j,k] = coslat_e[j] a(i) b(k), i.e. V0 cos(phi_e) with V0 = 1. It VANISHES AT THE POLES like cos(phi), which is the condition under which the operator's O(dphi/cos(phi)) truncation error converges POINTWISE (see the metadata). It is built from coslat_e itself, so the wind and the metric share the identical double. b(k) = cos(pi (k-1)/3) reverses sign with height, exercising BOTH donor branches; a(i) = 1 + 0.25 cos(2 pi (i-1/2)/8) is strictly positive. The pole faces V[i,1,k] and V[i,NLAT+1,k] are NEVER READ by the rule.",
          "expression": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "args": [
              "coslat_e"
            ],
            "output_idx": [
              "iv",
              "jv",
              "kv"
            ],
            "ranges": {
              "iv": {
                "from": "lon"
              },
              "jv": {
                "from": "lat_nodes"
              },
              "kv": {
                "from": "lev"
              }
            },
            "expr": {
              "op": "*",
              "args": [
                {
                  "op": "index",
                  "args": [
                    "coslat_e",
                    "jv"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "op": "+",
                  "args": [
                    1,
                    {
                      "op": "*",
                      "args": [
                        0.25,
                        {
                          "op": "cos",
                          "args": [
                            {
                              "op": "*",
                              "args": [
                                6.283185307179586,
                                {
                                  "op": "/",
                                  "args": [
                                    {
                                      "op": "-",
                                      "args": [
                                        "iv",
                                        0.5
                                      ]
                                    },
                                    8
                                  ]
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "op": "cos",
                  "args": [
                    {
                      "op": "*",
                      "args": [
                        3.141592653589793,
                        {
                          "op": "/",
                          "args": [
                            {
                              "op": "-",
                              "args": [
                                "kv",
                                1
                              ]
                            },
                            3
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        "src": {
          "type": "observed",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "MMS forcing: the EXACT cell-averaged flux divergence ( Phi[j+1] - Phi[j] ) / dS_lat[j] of the exact face flux Phi = V cos(phi) q* = (1 - s^2)(2 + s) a(i) b(k), s = sin(phi_e). Because Phi is a POLYNOMIAL IN sin(phi_e) it is EXACTLY 0.0 at both pole faces, so this single aggregate reproduces the rule's structural pole-term omission BITWISE (x - 0.0 = x; 0.0 - x = -x). It makes u* an exact steady state, so du/dt at t = 0 is precisely the operator's truncation error.",
